    "knowledgeBaseRetrievedContents": [
      "{FileName: knight-law-group-faqs-14-07-2026.md}\nThe CA Lemon Law changed on July 1, 2025, and the car manufacturers that refused to opt-in to the new law are the companies we can still sue on your behalf if you no longer have possession of the vehicle.\nIf your car manufacturer is one that opted into the new law, then unfortunately we cannot help you.\nHere is a list of the opt-ins and opt-outs:\n**Opted Into the New Law:** Alfa Romeo, Buick, Cadillac, Chevrolet, Chrysler, Dodge, Fiat, Ford, GMC, Hummer, Infiniti, Jaguar, Jeep, Kia, Land Rover, Lincoln, Maserati, Mercedes, Mercury, Mitsubishi, Nissan, Pontiac, RAM, Saturn, Smart, Hyundai, Subaru, Genesis, VinFast\n**Not Opted In (Old Law Applies):** Acura, Audi, Bentley, Harley Davidson, Lexus, Mazda, Mini, Porsche, Rolls Royce, Scion, Suzuki, Tesla, Toyota, Volkswagen, Volvo, Honda, BMW, Polestar, McLaren, Fisker\n---\n14. **Can you recommend me to another firm or lawyer?**\n**A:** Unfortunately, we are unable to provide any referrals.\nYou might consider utilizing search engines like Google or Yelp to seek assistance tailored to your situation.\n---\n15. **My car is not paid off.\nWhat happens to the amount that I owe?**\n**A:** If we can assist, the manufacturer will be responsible for settling the outstanding amount.\nYou would receive a refund of what you’ve paid, while the car manufacturer is responsible for the repayment to the lender.\n---\n16. **How long do these cases take?**\n**A:** Each situation is unique, and because we are fighting for the biggest outcomes, we want you to prepare for a potentially lengthy process.\nTypically, these cases can average between 18 to 24 months.\nThe timeline varies widely depending on how far the car manufacturer is willing to go.\n---\n17. **Do I continue making my car payments?**\n**A:** Yes, you want to continue making payments to your lender.\nYour lender is not the car manufacturer, and they are not the ones that sold you a defective vehicle.",
      "{FileName: KnightLaw_FAQs.md}\n{H1: Frequently Asked Questions (Initial Intake Call)}\n### How long does it take to review the repair documents?\n\n**A:** We can review your documents within 24-48 hours.\n### I think I had a lemon, but I sold the car. Can I still pursue my California Lemon Law rights?\n\n**A:** It depends on who your car manufacturer is. The CA Lemon Law changed on July 1, 2025, and the car manufacturers that refused to opt-in to the new law are the companies we can still sue on your behalf if you no longer have possession of the vehicle. If your car manufacturer is one that opted into the new law, then unfortunately we cannot help you.\nHere is a list of the opt-ins and opt-outs:",
      "{FileName: KnightLaw_FAQs.md}\n{H1: Representation Agreement - FAQs}\n### What is “good mileage”?\n\nThis refers to the mileage on your vehicle at the time of your first repair attempt. Manufacturers are entitled to deduct these miles from your buyback amount.\n### Do I need to keep the vehicle?\n\nIf your vehicle is from an “Opt-in” manufacturer under the new law from July 2025, then Yes. You must keep the vehicle until the claim is filed and for 60 days after. If you wish to sell, trade, or dispose of it, notify us 60 days in advance. If your vehicle is from an “Opt-out” manufacturer, no you do not need to maintain possession before your case is filed."
    ],
